\statement{A.81}{Proved}{Actual central compatibility and ordinary full realization do not restore an involution, even without cusp or frame normalization}
Let \(q:\Pi\twoheadrightarrow Q\), \(h_0\), and
\(b_0=h_0c_Q\) be the specified actual finite data of A.79,
and put \(L=\ker q\). For infinitely many primes
\(p\nmid2|Q|\), there are positive peripheral actions
\(h_p\) on the actual finite quotient
\[
 N_p=\overline{[L,L]L^p},\qquad G_p=\Pi/N_p
 \tag{A.81.1}
\]
with all the following properties. Their negative companions
\(b_p=h_pc_{G_p}\) are involutions fixing the literal
negative cusp and admitting both norm-one normalizer frames
above the prescribed Q-frames. The action extends to an
involution on the \emph{actual first source-central refinement}
\[
                         D_p=\Pi/\overline{[N_p,\Pi]},
 \tag{A.81.2}
\]
again with literal cusp inversion and both norm-one frames.
The corresponding positive action satisfies the ordinary
lifting criterion: some full positive peripheral h induces
\(h_p\), and h may fix \(\tau\) literally.

Nevertheless, no full negative peripheral involution induces
\(b_p\), even if its full cusp is only inverted up to
conjugacy and no full normalizer frame is prescribed. Thus
none of the ordinary full lifts of \(h_p\) can have
involutory negative companion. This assertion concerns a
constructed family at infinitely many eligible fresh primes;
no particular such prime or enormous coefficient matrix is
claimed to have been computed. Compatibility at every
iterated central refinement is not asserted. The surviving
\(W=0\) choices and the main existential problem remain open.

\begin{proof}
The proof identifies the new central obstruction exactly and
chooses the coefficient action to remove it. Throughout,
commutators are \([x,y]=xyx^{-1}y^{-1}\).

\emph{The compact-surface module admits an anti-symplectic
involution over algebraic numbers.}\par
Work first over \(k=\overline{\mathbb Q}\). Let
\(\mathscr M\) be the first homology of the actual
torsion-free modular surface cover, \(\mathscr B\) its
boundary submodule, and
\(\mathscr H=\mathscr M/\mathscr B\) its compact-surface
homology with the Q-invariant nondegenerate intersection
form. By the actual source sequences in A.80,
\[
 [\mathscr H]=2[1]+[kQ]
 -[k(Q/\langle s_0\rangle)]-[k(Q/\langle r_0\rangle)]
 -[k(Q/\langle t\rangle)],
 \tag{A.81.3}
\]
where \(t=q(\tau)\). The trivial multiplicity is zero.
For a nontrivial irreducible Q-module S its multiplicity is
\[
 m_S=\dim S-\dim S^{s_0}-\dim S^{r_0}-\dim S^t.
 \tag{A.81.4}
\]
Duality and twisting by \(b_0\) commute on the simple
modules. The fixed-space dimensions in (A.81.4) are unchanged
by either operation. We construct a \(b_0\)-semilinear
involution \(B_{\mathscr H}\) which negates the actual
intersection form, treating their joint orbits.

On four distinct constituents \(S,S^*,S^{b_0},(S^*)^{b_0}\),
choose a twisted isomorphism between the two dual pairs,
use the negative adjoint on the dual partner, and use the
inverse on the return maps. This gives an anti-symplectic
involution. If \(S\not\simeq S^*\) and \(b_0\) fixes
both classes, choose an intertwiner A on S and rescale it
to have \(A^2=1\). Use A on its multiplicity block and
\(-(A^{-1})^*\) on its dual block.

Suppose next that \(S\not\simeq S^*\) and
\(S^{b_0}\simeq S^*\). There is a nondegenerate twisted
bilinear form F, unique up to scalar, satisfying
\[
                    F(qx,b_0(q)y)=F(x,y).
 \tag{A.81.5}
\]
Its transpose is either F or \(-F\). In the symmetric
case, tensor it with a symmetric multiplicity form. In the
alternating case we need \(m_S\) even, as follows.
The actual norm-one lower frames make
\[
 F_s(x,y)=F(x,\beta_0y),\qquad F_r(x,y)=F(x,J_0y)
 \tag{A.81.6}
\]
alternating and nondegenerate: transposition uses
\(b_0(\beta_0)=\beta_0^{-1}\) and
\(b_0(J_0)=J_0^{-1}\). Moreover, \(s_0\) is an
isometry for \(F_s\), whereas \(r_0\) is self-adjoint
for \(F_r\) and t is self-adjoint for F. For example,
\[
 F_r(r_0x,r_0^{-1}y)=F_r(x,y),\qquad
 F(tx,t^{-1}y)=F(x,y).
\]
The finite-order operators are semisimple. Their fixed
subspaces are nondegenerate for these alternating forms:
in the self-adjoint cases distinct eigenspaces are
orthogonal, and in the isometric case the eigenvalue-one
space pairs only with itself. Thus all four dimensions on
the right of (A.81.4) are even, and \(m_S\) is even.
Tensor F with an alternating multiplicity form. In both
cases the resulting twisted form on \(X=S^{m_S}\) is
symmetric. If \(A:X\to X^*\) is its matrix, then
\[
                     (x,\phi)\longmapsto(A^{-1}\phi,Ax)
 \tag{A.81.7}
\]
on \(X\oplus X^*\) is the required anti-symplectic
semilinear involution.

Suppose S is self-dual and its class is fixed by \(b_0\).
Let its invariant form F have symmetry
\(\varepsilon\in\{1,-1\}\). Rescale an intertwiner A
so that \(A^2=1\). Its multiplier on F is then
\(\nu\in\{1,-1\}\). The actual symplectic isotypic
block has form \(F\otimes G\), where G has symmetry
\(-\varepsilon\). Seek \(A\otimes J\). If
\(\nu=-1\), take \(J=1\). If \(\nu=1\), take an
involutory anti-isometry of G. For alternating G this
exists on a symplectic basis. For symmetric G it exists
over k when its dimension is even, by a hyperbolic basis.
In this latter case F is alternating; every fixed space
of a finite-order Q-element is nondegenerate alternating,
so (A.81.4) again proves the required even dimension.
Finally, when \(b_0\) exchanges two distinct self-dual
classes, choose a twisted anti-isometry between their
isotypic blocks and its inverse. The invariant forms of
each given symmetry and dimension are isometric over k;
a square root of \(-1\) changes an isometry to an
anti-isometry if necessary. These cases exhaust the joint
duality and twisting orbits.

All constructions use algebraic numbers. Extend
\(\mathscr B\) by the signed cusp permutation of A.80
and choose a rational Q-splitting of
\(\mathscr M\to\mathscr H\). We obtain B on
\(\mathscr M\), defined over a number field, with
\[
 B^2=1,\qquad B(qv)=b_0(q)Bv,
 \qquad B|_{\mathscr B}\text{ is the negative cusp permutation},
 \tag{A.81.8}
\]
and with anti-symplectic induced action on \(\mathscr H\).
Semisimplicity here is over the stated field, as in
\citet[Theorem~7.4]{AMilne2025}; the preceding form and
parity argument is additional to A.80's module extension.

\emph{Choose eligible fresh primes.}\par
Take a finite Galois number field K containing the entries
of B and the finitely many basis changes just used. Avoid
their denominators and determinants, and avoid \(2|Q|\).
For clarity, existence of infinitely many completely split
primes needs no density estimate. Choose an integral
primitive element of K with monic minimal polynomial f
and \(f(0)\ne0\). For any finite excluded set of primes
\(\mathcal S\), enlarge it to contain the prime divisors
of \(\operatorname{disc}(f)\) and the preceding bad
integers. Put \(m_{\mathcal S}=\prod_{\ell\in\mathcal S}\ell\)
and \(A=f(0)m_{\mathcal S}\). The nonconstant polynomial
\[
                 g(X)=f(AX)/f(0)\in\mathbb Z[X]
                 \quad\text{satisfies}\quad
                 g(X)\equiv1\pmod{m_{\mathcal S}}.
\]
A prime divisor p of an integer \(|g(n)|>1\) is outside
\(\mathcal S\), and f has a root modulo p. Since
\(p\nmid\operatorname{disc}(f)\), this gives an unramified
degree-one prime of K over p. Galoisness makes all residue
degrees one, so p splits completely. Applying this to
arbitrary finite excluded sets proves the assertion;
compare the standard stronger statement in
\citet[Theorem~8.31 and Corollary~8.32]{AMilneANT2020}.
Reduction at such a prime gives the actual module
\[
 M=H_1(L,\mathbb F_p),\qquad
 P=\langle Q\cdot[\tau^m]\rangle\subset M,
 \qquad H=M/P,
 \tag{A.81.9}
\]
where \(m=\operatorname{ord}(t)\), and an operator B
satisfying (A.81.8) modulo p, with anti-symplectic
action on H. The integral boundary submodule and compact
homology are torsion-free, so the avoided basis
determinants ensure these are the actual mod-p source
modules and forms. This is an existence construction at
infinitely many fresh primes, not a numerical selection
of p or a stored matrix.

\emph{Identify the actual new central p-kernel.}\par
Put \(N=N_p\), \(Z=N/\overline{[N,\Pi]}\), and
let \(E_p\) be the actual quotient of \(D_p\) obtained
by killing the prime-to-p factors of Z. A.79's transfer
argument shows that \(D_p\) and Z are finite. Write
\(A=\ker(E_p\to G_p)\), and let \(\widetilde P\)
be the preimage of M. Its extension by A is central.
The source module has \(M_Q=0\), since its trivial
multiplicity in (A.80.8) is zero.

The kernel of \(\widetilde P^{\rm ab}\to M\) is
Q-trivial. Coinvariants are exact on finite abelian
p-groups when \(p\nmid|Q|\). A nonzero kernel would
therefore give a nonzero Q-invariant character of
\(\widetilde P\), hence of L, to \(C_p\), contrary
to \(M_Q=0\). It follows that
\[
              \widetilde P^{\rm ab}=M,
              \qquad A=\widetilde P',
              \qquad A\text{ is a quotient of }
                    W=(\bigwedge^2_{\mathbb F_p}M)_Q.
 \tag{A.81.10}
\]
The central commutator group thus has exponent p. The
pth-power map \(M\to A\) is additive for odd p,
since the class-two cross term has coefficient
\(p(p-1)/2\). It is Q-equivariant with trivial target,
so it vanishes. Hence \(\widetilde P\) has exponent p.

The upper bound is attained by an actual source quotient.
Define \(\mathcal E(M)=M\times W\) with law
\[
                 (a,z)(b,w)=(a+b,z+w+\tfrac12(a\wedge b)_Q),
 \tag{A.81.11}
\]
and give Q its natural action on M and trivial action on
W. Split \(G_p=M\rtimes Q\), and write its actual
source elements as \((a_s,s_0)\) and \((a_r,r_0)\).
In \(\mathcal E(M)\rtimes Q\) use the source images
\[
 S=(a_s,0,s_0),\qquad
 R=(a_r,-\tfrac16(a_r\wedge r_0a_r)_Q,r_0).
 \tag{A.81.12}
\]
They have orders two and three. Indeed the cube of
\((a_r,z,r_0)\) has central coordinate
\(3z+(a_r\wedge r_0a_r)_Q/2\). The source image
surjects \(G_p\); its L-image surjects M, so its
commutators attain the whole W. Thus the source image is
the entire displayed group. Together with (A.81.10),
this identifies the actual marked quotient
\[
                         E_p=\mathcal E(M)\rtimes Q.
 \tag{A.81.13}
\]

\emph{Remove precisely the central cusp discrepancy.}\par
An inner change of the entire marked model identification
over Q arranges
\[
             \tau=(a_0,z,t),\qquad ta_0=a_0,
             \qquad \tau^m=(ma_0,mz,1).
 \tag{A.81.14}
\]
Lift the required affine conjugation to \(\mathcal E(M)\)
and transport both source generators together. This changes
the model identification, not the actual source kernel;
the strict target law (A.81.11) remains the same.
Here \(a_0=[\tau^m]/m\) spans the identity boundary
line and \(P=\langle Q a_0\rangle\). The chosen B
sends \(a_0\) to \(-a_0\).

Let \(B_W\) be its induced exterior-square action on W.
The homogeneous action
\((a,w,q)\mapsto(Ba,B_Ww,b_0(q))\) is involutory,
and its central cusp discrepancy is
\(z_0=(1+B_W)z\). To locate it, kill P and
\((P\wedge M)_Q\). In this quotient the cusp is
\((0,\bar z,t)\). Every positive boundary generator
of L, conjugate to \(\tau^m\), has the same central
coordinate \(m\bar z\). The actual compact surface
relation therefore gives
\[
                 \Omega+|Q|\bar z=0
                 \quad\text{in }(\bigwedge^2H)_Q,
 \tag{A.81.15}
\]
where \(\Omega=\sum_i A_i\wedge B_i\) is the
symplectic bivector. This fixes the scalar and the
orientation convention; it does not discard boundary
cross terms before passing to the stated quotient.
The anti-symplectic action on H sends \(\Omega\) to
\(-\Omega\). Hence
\[
                         z_0\in I=(P\wedge M)_Q.
 \tag{A.81.16}
\]

The map
\[
                 M^t\longrightarrow I,
                 \qquad v\longmapsto(a_0\wedge v)_Q
 \tag{A.81.17}
\]
is onto. Move a Q-translate of \(a_0\) back to
\(a_0\) in coinvariants, and then average the other
argument over \(\langle t\rangle\). The map is
equivariant for \(-B\) on its source and \(B_W\)
on its target. Since \(B_Wz_0=z_0\), averaging
over \(C_2\) supplies \(v\in M^t\) with
\[
                         Bv=-v,
                 \qquad (a_0\wedge v)_Q=-z_0.
 \tag{A.81.18}
\]
Compose the homogeneous action with conjugation by
\((v,0,1)\). Its square is one, since
\(v+Bv=0\) and \(v\wedge Bv=0\). Direct use of
(A.81.11) changes the cusp discrepancy by
\(-(v\wedge a_0)_Q=(a_0\wedge v)_Q\).
Equation (A.81.18) therefore makes the actual cusp
literally inverse. Denote this involution on \(E_p\)
by \(\widetilde b_p\), and its image on \(G_p\)
by \(b_p\). The correction may change the finite
\(G_p\)-action; that action was a permitted choice
within the constructed family.

The lower s- and r-frames lift with their prescribed
Q-values. First lift them to \(G_p\) by A.80's
normalizer argument. Any further lift to \(E_p\)
gives the exact conjugacy equation: the two source
elements being compared differ by a central p-element
and have the same order two or three, so that element
is trivial. The normalizer square lies in W and is
fixed by \(\widetilde b_p\); its half corrects the
frame without changing its \(G_p\)-image. Thus both
frames are norm one in the actual \(E_p\).

\emph{Match the old central primes and apply the full
ordinary criterion.}\par
Let \(Q_1=\Pi/\overline{[L,\Pi]}\), the actual
old first central refinement. It is finite, and its
kernel over Q has exponent dividing \(6|Q|\), by
transfer. Thus \(|Q_1|\) is prime to p. For every
prime \(\ell\ne p\), restriction across
\(N\lhd L\), with \(L/N=M\) a p-group, gives
\[
 \operatorname{Hom}_\Pi(L,\mathbb Q_\ell/\mathbb Z_\ell)
       \simeq
 \operatorname{Hom}_\Pi(N,\mathbb Q_\ell/\mathbb Z_\ell).
 \tag{A.81.19}
\]
The positive-degree cohomology of M on these trivial
\(\ell\)-primary coefficients vanishes by averaging;
the inflation--restriction isomorphism is equivariant
for the outer \(\Pi/L\)-action, so taking invariants
gives (A.81.19). These are continuous characters.
Equivalently, the prime-to-p central parts of the two
actual source refinements agree. Together with
(A.81.13), this proves the actual fiber-product identity
\[
                         D_p=E_p\times_Q Q_1.
 \tag{A.81.20}
\]
The source image is full: over Q its two residual
kernels have coprime orders, and a subdirect product
of those kernels is their full product. If
\(d=1+|Q|/6\) and \(w=\dim_{\mathbb F_p}W\), this
also gives the explicit finite order
\[
                              |D_p|=|Q_1|p^{d+w}.
 \tag{A.81.21}
\]

A.79 independently supplies the old involutory
negative action on \(Q_1\), with literal cusp and
both norm-one frames above \(\beta_0,J_0\). Pair
it in (A.81.20) with \(\widetilde b_p\). Their
source actions agree on Q, and their two frames pair
over the same literal Q-values. This gives the
claimed negative involution and both frames on the
actual \(D_p\).

Canonical c preserves L, its characteristic subgroup
N, and \(\overline{[N,\Pi]}\). Composing the
constructed action on \(D_p\) with c gives a
positive peripheral automorphism fixing \(\tau\)
literally. The actual central criterion A.3, in its
closed-normal modular form audited after A.34, now
supplies a full ordinary positive automorphism inducing
\(h_p=b_pc_{G_p}\) on \(G_p\). An inner correction
in N fixes the full cusp literally if required.
This does not assert that the full lift induces the
entire chosen action on \(D_p\); the retained action
in that criterion is the one on \(G_p\).

Finally, (A.80.16)--(A.80.18) apply at every eligible
odd p. A full negative involution inducing \(b_p\)
would have cyclic cusp-conjugator image in Q and
could be normalized, preserving both its involutivity
and its Q-action. A.79 excludes the result. The
nonrealization conclusion is therefore independent
of all full cusp and frame normalizations, whereas
the new ordinary full positive realization has just
been proved separately. Only the first central
refinement (A.81.2) has been used.
\end{proof}
